// The web Orbit Lab update history, copied from index.html (SIMS['Orbit Lab'].changelog).
// Shown in the native changelog panel for reference. Generated once; regenerate if the web history changes.
    {"7.7.2", "Slower Journey",
     "The 🚀 Journey stayed on each stop for the same 3.4 seconds however long the text was, so the longer ones went past before you could read them. Each stop now stays as long as its text needs (about 2 seconds plus one second for every 18 letters, so 7 to 13 seconds), and the zoom between stops is about a third slower."},
    {"7.7.1", "Lighter drawing",
     "The orbit trails are drawn with less work: the part that trims each line to the screen now skips lines that are fully on screen and no longer makes throwaway lists. About 10 % less work per frame with 3D, full trails and the sky switched on. The picture is exactly the same, pixel for pixel."},
    {"7.7", "Faster at high time speed",
     "At the fastest time speeds the orbits are worked out in far less time, so the picture stays smooth on phones. Each small time step used to work out the Sun’s pull on every body twice; the pull at the end of one step is the same as at the start of the next, so it is now worked out once and reused. The physics is unchanged: the same method (leapfrog), the same step size and the same orbits."},
    {"7.6", "Help guide on one page, with a jump bar",
     "The “What am I even looking at?” guide is now one long page instead of dropdown boxes: every section is open, the headings stay as headings, and a row of tappable headings at the top jumps you to any section. Each heading also has a small ↑ that takes you back to that row. The text itself is unchanged."},
    {"7.5.1", "The Sun is called the Sun",
     "The label on the star now says “Sun · 1 solar mass (M☉)” instead of “Star · 1.00 Sun”, and its info box is titled “Sun · G-type star” and gives its mass in solar masses (M☉), the unit astronomers use: one solar mass is the mass of the Sun, 1.99×10³⁰ kg."},
    {"7.5", "Eight new timeline steps and “Why do we have seasons?”",
     "The Solar System timeline has eight new steps: Huygens and Saturn’s ring (1655–1659), Shapley showing the Sun is not at the centre of the galaxy (1918), landing on Venus and Mars (1970–1976), Comet Shoemaker–Levy 9 hitting Jupiter (1994), Voyager 1 leaving the Sun’s wind (2012), Philae landing on a comet and Dawn orbiting Ceres (2014–2015), Parker Solar Probe touching the Sun (2018–2021) and the 2020 Nobel Prize for weighing the black hole at the centre of our galaxy. Most have a ▶ button that flies to the place or jumps to the day. There is also a new guide in Learn, 🌍 Why do we have seasons?: the tilt of Earth’s axis, a diagram, and live numbers from the lab’s own Earth (jump to the real 2026 equinoxes and solstices and see day length and the noon Sun height from pole to pole), and how the tilt of each planet gives it seasons or none."},
    {"7.4.1", "Smoother zooming",
     "Zooming in and out of planets and the Sun dropped the frame rate in a few places. Fixed: (1) the round picture of a planet used to be rebuilt every few pixels while you zoomed; now it uses a few set sizes, borrows a nearby size while you are zooming, and builds the sharp one a moment after you stop. (2) A plain planet’s colour and shading are now painted in one go instead of two full-size fills. (3) The Sun’s glowing patches that are off the picture are skipped. (4) The date in the corner is only re-made once a day instead of every frame. Nothing looks different once it settles. Also new: clicking the galactic centre gives the Galactic Center achievement."},
    {"7.4", "The Sun really spins, seasons, galactic centre",
     "The Sun now turns the way the real one does: its axis is tilted 7.3° from upright (the IAU value for its north pole) and it turns once in 25.4 days, the standard rate. Its surface is gas, so the real equator turns faster (about 25 days) than the poles (about 34): the card says so. The glowing patches on it are decoration, but they turn with the real axis and rate. Earth’s card now says which season it is (north and south), how long ago it began and when the next starts, and where on Earth the Sun is straight overhead, all worked out from the real tilt and Earth’s place on its orbit. The black hole at the centre of our galaxy is now labelled Galactic centre when you zoom out to it."},
    {"7.3", "Info box smaller on phones",
     "On a phone the facts box still took up too much of the picture, because the picture is narrow and the text was the same size as on a big screen. The box now shrinks with the width of the picture: full size on a computer, about 25% smaller on a phone."},
    {"7.2", "Smoother when zoomed in on a planet",
     "Zooming in on a planet and turning it (or turning the 3D view) made the frame rate drop, because the picture of the spinning planet was worked out pixel by pixel every frame at full size. It is now much cheaper: the maths per pixel is faster, and while the planet or the view is moving a smaller picture is drawn (smooth), and a moment after it stops the sharp one is drawn. It looks the same once it settles."},
    {"7.1", "Smaller info box, safer first tap",
     "The facts box on the picture is now smaller and more compact (smaller text, tighter lines, and see-through), so the planet or object you are looking at stays visible. Its last line now says “tap anywhere to close this box”. And the first tap now only closes the box: before, tapping the picture while zoomed in close at a fast Time speed also let go of the planet and the view flew away. A second tap selects or lets go as before, and tapping a Go to tile or the Follow button works as always."},
    {"7", "Spinning planets, Journey and Jump to a date",
     "The planets now really spin. Each one turns at its real speed and leans the real way, from the IAU’s rotation data: Earth’s axis leans 23.4°, Uranus lies on its side (97.8°), Venus is upside down (177.4°) and turns backwards, and Saturn’s rings now tilt with its axis. A dashed line shows each planet’s axis, with N at the end it turns counter-clockwise around. Earth shows its real coastlines turned the way Earth really is on the date shown; the other planets are plain-coloured with a grid of latitude and longitude and one bright 0° meridian, so their turning can be seen. Pick a planet and tap ⏱ Slow time to watch it turn (one turn takes about 24 seconds). At fast Time speeds a planet would turn too fast to see, so it is held still. The planet cards now say how long one turn takes and how far the axis leans. A new Journey and dates card has two things. 🚀 Journey zooms out by itself from Earth and the Moon past the planets, Voyager 1, the Oort cloud and the nearest stars to the centre of our galaxy, with a short caption at each stop; touching anything stops it. Jump to a date: pick any date from 1800 to 2100 (Greenwich time) and every planet, dwarf planet, comet and belt moves to where it really was or will be (NASA/JPL orbit numbers for 1800–2050, a little less exact after that). It matches famous days: Mars is 55.8 million km away on 27 Aug 2003, and Jupiter and Saturn are 0.1° apart on 21 Dec 2020. Tiles jump to Halley at its closest (1986) and back (2061), Neptune’s discovery (1846), the 2012 transit of Venus, the 2020 great conjunction and Today. On a new date the moons start at made-up places, and the Voyagers show only from 1990. Also fixed: the 3D view was upside down (the north pole of the Solar System pointed down the screen, so Earth’s North Pole would have too). It is now the right way up, which turns the default 3D picture a half-turn. The date at the top left is now in Greenwich time."},
    {"6.1", "Easier to read",
     "The new night sky was drowning the planets, so: the stars are now dim up close and get brighter as you zoom out, where the sky is the point (the Stars setting is Soft by default, with Full for the old strength). Planet, comet and ring labels now have a thin dark edge so they stay readable on top of stars. The Voyagers no longer cross the planets at the normal view: they only appear once you zoom out past about ×0.03, or when you pick one. And when you are zoomed far out, a short line at the bottom left says how wide the picture is and that the whole Solar System is now the ringed dot in the middle."},
    {"6", "Real stars and far-away things",
     "Space is no longer empty. Behind the planets there is now the real night sky: the 2,874 brightest stars at their true directions and true colours (Sirius, Betelgeuse, the Pleiades’ neighbours and all the rest), with a soft band for the plane of the Milky Way. The sky is so far away that it does not move when you zoom, only when you turn the 3D view. A new Constellations setting draws the constellation lines and, if you want, their names and the brightest stars’ names. Zoom out much further (now to ×0.000000001) and the real far-away things appear at their true direction and distance: the nearest stars (Alpha Centauri at 4.3 light years, Barnard’s Star, Sirius, Vega and more), the Pleiades, the Orion Nebula and Sagittarius A*, the black hole at the centre of our galaxy, 27,000 light years away. Voyager 1 and Voyager 2 are there too, at their real place on 30 Sep 2026, moving at their real speeds as time runs, with the spot where each left the Sun’s wind marked. Click any of them for facts, including how long Voyager 1 would need to get there. The distance rings now count in light years as well, and new Go to tiles fly you to them. The picture now has a dark space background."},
    {"5.6", "View fixes and short tails",
     "Letting go, going to belts, Ceres and deep zoom, plus short tails. Tapping the tile you are on again (or the Follow button) now only removes the pink ring and stops following; the picture stays exactly where it was, where before in 3D it could shift to a different spot. Going to the asteroid belt, any belt or the Oort cloud used to leave 3D looking closer or further away depending on what you had looked at before; every Go to now starts from a clean view. The lag around Ceres at the 500 to 2,000 km scale came from the thin dotted line that drops from a planet down to the flat plane (millions of pixels long, drawn as millions of tiny dashes) and the faint green habitable zone band (billions of pixels wide); both are now drawn only where you can see them, and planets fully off the picture are skipped. Zooming far into a planet, and out from the asteroid belt, is lighter on the graphics card: the planet, its pink ring, its moons’ orbits and its trail are drawn only where you can see them, and each belt is drawn in one go. Every planet, dwarf planet and comet now has a short tail behind it instead of a trail that grows over many laps, and a new Trails setting in the View card switches between “Short tail” (the default) and “Full orbit trails”. It all looks the same, only smoother."},
    {"5.5", "What am I even looking at?",
     "A new ❓ button at the top opens a plain-words guide to the picture, in tap-to-open sections: what each ring, dot, line and coloured ball means, the pink ring, what Time speed means, what is to scale and what is only made to be seen, why the Sun wobbles (the barycentre), and comets and their tails. The text follows your 2D/3D and True scale / Easy to see choice, and shows live facts about the date and the thing you have selected."},
    {"5.1", "Comets stay on their line, smooth zoom near the Sun",
     "Fixed a big slowdown: zoomed in close to the Sun, the comets’ dotted orbits stretched millions of pixels off screen and the app drew all of it every frame. Now only the part you can see is drawn, and the frame rate stays high everywhere. When you zoomed far in on a comet, its dotted orbit was drawn as straight pieces that were far too long, so the comet slid off the line and seemed to bounce at fast Time speeds. The orbit is now drawn with points packed tightly around the comet itself, so it sits exactly on the line at every zoom (checked on all six comets: 0 pixels off, before it was up to thousands)."},
    {"5", "Scale bar and famous comets",
     "A scale bar at the bottom left of the picture shows how long a round distance is at this zoom (in km, million km or AU) and how long light takes to cross it: 8.3 minutes for 1 AU, about 1.3 seconds for the Moon’s orbit. Six famous comets have arrived: Halley, Hale–Bopp, NEOWISE, Encke, 67P (Rosetta) and Swift–Tuttle. Each follows its real orbit (dotted line), sits where it really is today, and grows a glowing head and two tails once the Sun warms it: a straight blue gas tail and a curved yellow dust tail, both pointing away from the Sun. Their cards give the last and next closest approach. There is a new Comets section in Go to and a Famous comets switch under Solar System extras. Comet orbits are drawn from NASA/JPL data; Jupiter nudges them, so real passage dates can shift a little."},
    {"4.6", "Time speed up top",
     "The Time speed slider is now the second card on the right, right under Reset to today, so you can change the speed without scrolling down past the Go to tiles."},
    {"4.5", "True scale, deeper zoom, real Earth",
     "Planets, moons and the Sun are now drawn at their TRUE size. At the wide view they are still small dots so you can find them, but zoom in and they grow to their real size next to their real distances: the Sun is a small ball, Earth is tiny next to the Moon’s orbit, and the barycentre now sits just outside the Sun’s edge. The zoom now goes 4 times deeper (up to ×300,000), enough to fill the picture with Earth or Mercury. Go to tiles pick a zoom for you: a planet tile shows the planet up close, a moon tile shows that moon’s whole orbit, and the Sun tile shows the Sun. A new Sizes setting switches back to the old “Easy to see” sizes (planets drawn bigger, moon orbits drawn wider). Earth also has real continents now: green land, deserts, ice caps, clouds and a thin blue edge of air. In True scale there is no longer a minimum dot size: the Sun, planets and moons are exactly as big as they really are at every zoom. When one is smaller than a pixel (which is most of the time when zoomed out) a small ring marks where it is, so you can still find and tap it. The Easy to see setting keeps the old dots."},
    {"4.1", "Barycentre",
     "A new Barycentre marker (a crosshair in a ring) and a Barycentre tile in the Go to card. The barycentre is the balance point of the Sun and all the planets, worked out from their real masses. The whole picture is now drawn around it, so the Sun is the one that wobbles, mostly because of Jupiter. Real barycentre sits from inside the Sun out to about 2 Sun radii. The marker is hidden at wide zoom, where it would sit on the Sun’s centre; use the Go to tile or zoom in on the Sun."},
    {"4", "Real-looking planets and Sun",
     "Planets, moons and the Sun are lit balls (bright on the side facing the Sun, dark on the far side), with a glowing Sun that turns about once in 25 days and wobbles a little around the centre of the system. Stripes, continents, clouds, craters and sunspots were tried and taken away again, so each body is one smooth colour; Saturn keeps its rings passing in front of and behind it. The Sun’s black dots gave way to soft bright and orange patches of glowing gas that still turn with it. The Orbit Lab now starts at 1 s = 29 days instead of 291 days, so the inner planets no longer go crazy, and moons keep true time at every speed (only when a lap takes less than about eight frames is the moon’s whole orbit lit up instead of a dot, and its card says how many laps a second it does)."},
    {"3.1", "Go to polish",
     "Trails of far-away bodies (the dwarf planets most of all) used to stop a little short of the body and then jump forward every second or so; the line now always reaches the body, so it moves smoothly. The note at the top left now shows the date as time passes (for example 29 Sep 2126 after 100 years) and how long it has been since today, instead of the starting date only. Going to a planet, moon or belt no longer changes the time speed: it stays where you put it. The mouse wheel now zooms in smaller steps, so it is easy to stop on a far-away dwarf planet without moving the picture after every scroll. The ring that marks what you flew to is now bright pink with a dark edge, so it stands out from orange and beige planets. When you go to a belt or the Oort cloud, all of its rocks light up pink and get a bit bigger, and its name gets a pink tag, so you can see exactly which dots are the belt. Tap the tile you are on in the Go to card again and it lets go: the pink ring goes away and the picture stops following it. A little x y z compass now sits at the bottom right of the picture (above the info card when one is open). It turns as you turn the 3D view; in the flat view z points at you, so it is drawn as a dot."},
    {"3", "Go to table",
     "A new Go to card with a tile for the Sun, every planet, the five dwarf planets, all fourteen moons, the asteroid belt, Jupiter’s Trojans, the Kuiper belt and the Oort cloud, coloured like the bodies themselves. Tap one to fly there: it gets selected and stays in the middle of the picture, and for planets with moons and for moons, time slows down so you can watch them. On a phone the picture scrolls into view. The tile of whatever you have selected is highlighted, also when you click it in the picture."},
    {"2.5", "Just the real Solar System, timeline and real time",
     "The lab now opens on the real Solar System, with everything where it is today, and nothing else: the custom star, the Add / Remove planet buttons, the New planet launch controls and the Inner planets preset are gone. The one preset button is now ☉ Reset to today, which puts everything back where it is today and resets the view and time speed. New Learn card with a 21-step timeline: wandering stars, Aristarchus and Eratosthenes, Ptolemy, Copernicus, Kepler, Galileo, measuring the AU, Newton, Halley, Uranus and Neptune, Ceres, Pluto, the Space Age, Voyager, the Kuiper belt, the first exoplanet, Titan, New Horizons, ʻOumuamua, DART and the Oort cloud. Many steps have a ▶ button that takes the lab there (Jupiter’s moons, Mars’s orbit, Pluto, the Kuiper belt, the Oort cloud …). Time speed now goes all the way down to 1 s = 1 second (real time), from 1 s = 1 second up through minutes, hours, days and years. The timeline’s ▶ buttons no longer switch you from 3D to 2D: they keep the view you chose. In 3D, a planet’s moons could disappear at some viewing angles (Earth’s Moon and the moons of Mars, and Jupiter’s when zoomed out a bit further). Whether the moons are shown now depends only on how far you have zoomed in, not on the angle, so they stay put while you rotate."},
    {"2", "Real Solar System, moons, belts and dwarf planets",
     "One click puts Mercury to Neptune where they really are today (NASA/JPL orbital elements), with real sizes, masses, gravity and temperatures. New time-speed slider and a much wider zoom-out. Fourteen real moons (Moon, Phobos, Deimos, the four Galilean moons, Titan, Rhea, Enceladus, Titania, Oberon, Triton, Charon), the asteroid belt with its gaps, Jupiter's Trojans, the Kuiper belt, and the dwarf planets Ceres, Pluto, Haumea, Makemake and Eris. Click any of them for facts. Zoom way out in the Real Solar System to reach the Oort cloud: a flattened inner part (2,000 – 20,000 AU) and a round outer part out to about 200,000 AU. Rings and labels now work at any zoom."},
    {"1.5", "Move the view and click for info",
     "Drag to move the view (right-drag or Shift-drag in 3D). Click a planet or the star for size, mass, gravity, distance, speed, orbit, lap time and temperature. Follow button, habitable-zone band, coloured star, planet types."},
    {"1", "First version, 3D view and real units",
     "A star with planets, name labels, add / remove planets, circular and escape speed readout, planets removed when they crash or escape. Each lab now shows its own update number. Click the Update badge to see this list. New 3D view: drag to rotate, orbits can be tilted, depth shown by size, drop lines and a ground grid. Zoom with scroll, pinch or the slider (also in 2D). Star mass in Suns, distance in AU (1 AU = Earth to Sun), speed in km/s, time in years. Labelled distance rings, a \"Circular speed\" button, and a live line saying what the launch will do (circle, ellipse or escape)."},
